WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.959 --> 00:00:07.360
How can an influential
and exclusive business

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:09.393
become more inclusive?

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:13.873
Educator and disability
advocate Sinéad Burke

00:00:13.873 --> 00:00:15.970
is on a mission to change the way

00:00:15.970 --> 00:00:18.243
fashion is represented in our world.

00:00:20.103 --> 00:00:22.000
When this process first began,

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:24.460
which was almost two years ago,

00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:26.050
it was for a need and an urgency for

00:00:26.050 --> 00:00:27.770
the National Museums of Scotland

00:00:27.770 --> 00:00:29.791
because they were doing this exhibition

00:00:29.791 --> 00:00:32.120
on the diversity of fashion.

00:00:32.120 --> 00:00:35.920
Sinéad has achondroplasia,
a form of dwarfism,

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:38.280
and identifies as a little person.

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:41.215
I asked how they were going
to exhibit my clothes.

00:00:41.215 --> 00:00:43.844
They said that they were gonna
hang them from the ceiling.

00:00:43.844 --> 00:00:46.380
I asked how everything else
was going to be displayed

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:49.020
and they said, well, mannequins.

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:51.117
So really, almost flippantly, I said,

00:00:51.117 --> 00:00:53.317
"Okay, well how do we
get a mannequin made?"

00:00:55.723 --> 00:00:56.920
How cool is that?

00:00:56.920 --> 00:00:57.753
It's amazing.

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:02.262
I met with experts,
executives, and advocates

00:01:02.262 --> 00:01:05.200
in London and Paris who want to see

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:08.730
the gilded doors of the
fashion business thrown open.

00:01:08.730 --> 00:01:11.940
I certainly experienced tremendous racism.

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:14.930
I didn't fit the very narrow profile

00:01:14.930 --> 00:01:16.627
of what was acceptable.

00:01:16.627 --> 00:01:19.550
Together, we're going to explore not just

00:01:19.550 --> 00:01:23.052
the moral argument, but the business case

00:01:23.052 --> 00:01:25.053
for letting everyone in.

00:01:28.105 --> 00:01:31.085
What we wear says a lot about who we are,

00:01:31.085 --> 00:01:35.766
yet fashion is also a $2.5
trillion global industry

00:01:35.766 --> 00:01:38.223
that touches everyone on Earth.

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:41.320
I'm Imran Amed.

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:42.910
I first started trying to make sense

00:01:42.910 --> 00:01:45.430
of the fashion business 15 years ago

00:01:45.430 --> 00:01:48.040
as it was being transformed by technology,

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:51.323
globalization, and
shifting consumer values.

00:01:52.542 --> 00:01:54.750
Now I'm on a journey to see how fashion

00:01:54.750 --> 00:01:56.966
is recalibrating amid the pandemic

00:01:56.966 --> 00:02:00.044
to balance profit with purpose.

00:02:00.044 --> 00:02:02.550
This is "The Business of Fashion" show.

00:02:02.550 --> 00:02:05.510
Join me to discover how
fashion shapes business,

00:02:05.510 --> 00:02:07.324
culture, and identity,

00:02:07.324 --> 00:02:10.793
and to meet the people
forging fashion's future.

00:02:17.114 --> 00:02:20.723
The luxury business is
built on exclusivity.

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:26.220
Fashion can appear inclusive
in terms of ethnicity,

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:28.233
body shape, and age.

00:02:29.386 --> 00:02:32.973
And designers may have shone
a broader light on beauty,

00:02:34.688 --> 00:02:38.203
but a caution about always
taking fashion at face value.

00:02:39.182 --> 00:02:42.270
Analysis of thousands of images posted by

00:02:42.270 --> 00:02:44.305
leading luxury brands on Instagram

00:02:44.305 --> 00:02:46.810
revealed that light skinned models

00:02:46.810 --> 00:02:49.640
continue to dominate fashion imagery.

00:02:49.640 --> 00:02:52.192
And when it comes to
decision makers at the top,

00:02:52.192 --> 00:02:56.784
in the design studio or the boardroom,

00:02:56.784 --> 00:03:00.403
the seats of power
remain largely unchanged.

00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:05.253
It's good to witness
visible and vocal advocates,

00:03:06.260 --> 00:03:11.260
from trans icons to gender
nonconforming people.

00:03:11.930 --> 00:03:14.733
Fashion is doing more
to count everyone in,

00:03:15.880 --> 00:03:17.480
yet the business case for creating

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:20.230
more diversity inside fashion companies

00:03:20.230 --> 00:03:22.356
is even more powerful,

00:03:22.356 --> 00:03:26.210
leading to higher levels of profitability.

00:03:26.210 --> 00:03:27.890
Businesses with the most ethnic

00:03:27.890 --> 00:03:31.175
and gender diversity
outperform the least diverse

00:03:31.175 --> 00:03:34.803
by as much as 36%

00:03:34.803 --> 00:03:37.770
and they are much more
likely to tilt the lens

00:03:37.770 --> 00:03:39.840
to spot new business opportunities

00:03:41.343 --> 00:03:44.730
such as a largely untapped
15% of the world's

00:03:44.730 --> 00:03:46.850
population who have disabilities

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:51.113
and a spending power
topping $1.2 trillion.

00:03:52.310 --> 00:03:54.210
No wonder fashion is paying attention.

00:03:55.673 --> 00:03:58.086
The wider positive trend,

00:03:58.086 --> 00:04:01.943
boardroom diversity widens
the perspective at the top.

00:04:02.950 --> 00:04:04.960
Entry-level pathways are no longer

00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:07.990
reserved only for those
who can afford to take them

00:04:09.410 --> 00:04:11.300
because the bottom line,

00:04:11.300 --> 00:04:14.511
to the benefit of shareholders
in society at large

00:04:14.511 --> 00:04:17.993
is that belonging is good business.

00:04:25.314 --> 00:04:28.920
Since this is an episode about belonging,

00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:30.600
it's important to say a few things

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:32.613
about my own identity upfront.

00:04:34.180 --> 00:04:37.263
I am am Ismaili-Muslim
man of Indian origin,

00:04:38.660 --> 00:04:41.593
born in Canada to
immigrants from East Africa.

00:04:43.100 --> 00:04:44.720
I was an outsider when I started

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:47.890
sneaking into fashion shows in 2005.

00:04:47.890 --> 00:04:50.730
Back then, the front rows were a uniform,

00:04:50.730 --> 00:04:53.083
narrow representation of the world.

00:04:58.432 --> 00:05:02.130
Now, I am invited into
elite fashion spaces

00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:04.670
where the front rows are changing.

00:05:04.670 --> 00:05:07.270
One stalwart is Edward Enninful,

00:05:07.270 --> 00:05:09.360
the first Black man to be appointed as

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:11.973
the Editor in Chief of
Conde Nast British Vogue.

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:15.220
At the Paris couture shows,

00:05:15.220 --> 00:05:17.980
I was also reunited with Samira Nasr

00:05:17.980 --> 00:05:20.690
who became Editor in
Chief of Harper's Bazaar,

00:05:20.690 --> 00:05:24.346
owned by fashion's other
media giant, Hearst.

00:05:24.346 --> 00:05:26.250
After a long day of shows,

00:05:26.250 --> 00:05:28.070
we met to talk about everything that has

00:05:28.070 --> 00:05:30.474
happened since the
beginning of the pandemic,

00:05:30.474 --> 00:05:34.593
including her rise to the
very top of fashion media.

00:05:36.670 --> 00:05:38.860
Her premiere issue was poignant,

00:05:38.860 --> 00:05:41.833
examining the paradox of being first.

00:05:44.450 --> 00:05:46.210
Your appointment in this role was

00:05:46.210 --> 00:05:48.190
not just a big change for you,

00:05:48.190 --> 00:05:50.726
it represented pretty significant shift

00:05:50.726 --> 00:05:54.600
for Harper's Bazaar, for fashion media,

00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:57.311
the first woman of color ever in the

00:05:57.311 --> 00:06:00.594
history of Harper's Bazaar at the helm.

00:06:00.594 --> 00:06:02.440
What did it mean to you personally,

00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:03.860
but what do you think it also signified

00:06:03.860 --> 00:06:06.010
for all those young women from

00:06:06.010 --> 00:06:08.610
different backgrounds who never saw

00:06:08.610 --> 00:06:10.504
someone like you in a role like this?

00:06:10.504 --> 00:06:15.460
I think that in that
moment, everyone felt seen.

00:06:15.460 --> 00:06:17.629
I think it was so much about me and

00:06:17.629 --> 00:06:19.858
people rooting for me,
but I think ultimately,

00:06:19.858 --> 00:06:23.980
people were also rooting
for their likeness.

00:06:23.980 --> 00:06:26.790
It's an acknowledgement of all people

00:06:26.790 --> 00:06:29.960
who have felt not seen and it just means

00:06:29.960 --> 00:06:31.660
that more people can dream and aspire

00:06:31.660 --> 00:06:33.460
to something that they
never thought they could,

00:06:33.460 --> 00:06:35.740
and that's a beautiful thing.

00:06:35.740 --> 00:06:39.470
What does real inclusion
look like in fashion?

00:06:39.470 --> 00:06:40.620
Walk down the street.

00:06:41.670 --> 00:06:44.120
To me, that's exactly what it should mean.

00:06:44.120 --> 00:06:45.380
You shouldn't be excluded because

00:06:45.380 --> 00:06:47.050
where you come from,
or what you look like,

00:06:47.050 --> 00:06:49.936
or your sexual orientation,
or how you identify yourself,

00:06:49.936 --> 00:06:52.210
it should just be are you
willing to do the work

00:06:52.210 --> 00:06:55.200
and do you have the passion to try.

00:06:55.200 --> 00:06:58.010
That's what every
industry should look like.

00:06:58.010 --> 00:07:01.600
Back in the days when you first
started, what was it like?

00:07:01.600 --> 00:07:05.110
It was hard and I certainly experienced

00:07:06.180 --> 00:07:07.573
tremendous racism.

00:07:09.950 --> 00:07:10.890
Within the industry?

00:07:10.890 --> 00:07:12.150
Oh my gosh, yeah.

00:07:12.150 --> 00:07:14.054
I've had some horrible things said to me.

00:07:14.054 --> 00:07:18.890
Having to go meet an editor,
a big, powerful editor

00:07:18.890 --> 00:07:21.480
and everyone's like okay,

00:07:21.480 --> 00:07:23.150
you have to go meet this editor and

00:07:23.150 --> 00:07:25.020
let's make sure you look okay and then

00:07:25.020 --> 00:07:30.020
someone says, so and so
editor doesn't like messy hair

00:07:30.998 --> 00:07:33.420
and so all the people who are around me

00:07:33.420 --> 00:07:36.280
who are trying to help me get
ready are like, oh my God.

00:07:36.280 --> 00:07:37.590
And I'm looking.

00:07:37.590 --> 00:07:39.630
Who has messy hair?

00:07:39.630 --> 00:07:40.463
Who has messy hair?

00:07:40.463 --> 00:07:43.160
And I realize 'cause my hair,
when it's out, it's very big,

00:07:43.160 --> 00:07:45.060
and I realized they're talking about me

00:07:45.060 --> 00:07:47.820
and that's just one example of

00:07:48.954 --> 00:07:52.662
my natural texture hair was messy.

00:07:52.662 --> 00:07:57.662
I didn't fit the very narrow
profile of what was acceptable.

00:07:57.942 --> 00:08:01.200
So now that you're in
the role that you're in,

00:08:01.200 --> 00:08:03.470
you must feel a great
sense of responsibility.

00:08:03.470 --> 00:08:05.770
This role isn't about me, it's about we.

00:08:05.770 --> 00:08:07.896
It's about the community
that I can bring together

00:08:07.896 --> 00:08:12.600
and share these platforms
and elevate these voices

00:08:12.600 --> 00:08:16.440
and bring them along with
me, so it's about the we.

00:08:16.440 --> 00:08:18.430
But I believe we all
have a responsibility.

00:08:18.430 --> 00:08:21.134
It's like you get through the door

00:08:21.134 --> 00:08:24.700
and every one of us
has a responsibility to

00:08:24.700 --> 00:08:27.010
look back from where you came and

00:08:27.010 --> 00:08:29.960
extend your hand and
pull someone in with you,

00:08:29.960 --> 00:08:32.310
but this industry isn't
entirely filled with

00:08:32.310 --> 00:08:33.190
horrible people.

00:08:33.190 --> 00:08:35.150
I feel like we have to
change that narrative also

00:08:35.150 --> 00:08:36.300
'cause I love this industry.

00:08:36.300 --> 00:08:38.070
Why do you love this industry?

00:08:38.070 --> 00:08:38.903
It's everything.

00:08:38.903 --> 00:08:42.638
It reflects our times, it is
a community, it's creative,

00:08:42.638 --> 00:08:44.870
it is a social commentary,

00:08:44.870 --> 00:08:47.230
it's kind of a social experiment, really.

00:08:47.230 --> 00:08:49.400
So many misfits who find their way in

00:08:49.400 --> 00:08:51.578
and find their voice and their place

00:08:51.578 --> 00:08:54.770
and we get to dream, you know?

00:08:54.770 --> 00:08:57.280
We saw two incredible shows today and

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:00.580
those people are part of my community.

00:09:00.580 --> 00:09:01.413
And we get to do that.

00:09:01.413 --> 00:09:02.590
And we get to do that.

00:09:02.590 --> 00:09:03.423
Cheers.

00:09:03.423 --> 00:09:04.256
Cheers.

00:09:05.400 --> 00:09:06.270
We'll always have couture.

00:09:06.270 --> 00:09:07.103
We will.

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:12.420
Samira's idea of using her influence

00:09:12.420 --> 00:09:14.800
to open doors for others sticks with me

00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:16.363
as I head back to London.

00:09:24.470 --> 00:09:27.530
Every year, "The Business of
Fashion" hosts a live event

00:09:27.530 --> 00:09:31.554
called "Voices", our annual
gathering for big thinkers.

00:09:31.554 --> 00:09:35.606
In 2017, disability advocate Sinead Burke

00:09:35.606 --> 00:09:38.090
addressed an audience of fashion leaders

00:09:38.090 --> 00:09:40.500
and explained how the
industry systematically

00:09:40.500 --> 00:09:42.660
excludes disabled people.

00:09:42.660 --> 00:09:44.955
I have had an insatiable interest

00:09:44.955 --> 00:09:48.780
in the fashion industry for
as long as I can remember

00:09:49.640 --> 00:09:52.632
despite having such limited access to it.

00:09:52.632 --> 00:09:55.820
As founder as CEO of "Tilting the Lens",

00:09:55.820 --> 00:09:59.501
a consultancy that brings
visibility to inaccessibility,

00:09:59.501 --> 00:10:02.230
Sinéad advises companies like Gucci

00:10:02.230 --> 00:10:06.253
and Ralph Lauren on moving
from awareness to action.

00:10:07.230 --> 00:10:08.850
When I meet with Sinéad in London,

00:10:08.850 --> 00:10:11.800
we start by reflecting on
her journey from "Voices"

00:10:11.800 --> 00:10:14.945
and how her goals have
changed along the way.

00:10:14.945 --> 00:10:17.773
When you meet someone now,

00:10:18.750 --> 00:10:23.083
how do you describe who
you are and what you do?

00:10:24.720 --> 00:10:25.553
It's funny.

00:10:25.553 --> 00:10:28.900
I think if we were having this
conversation five years ago,

00:10:28.900 --> 00:10:31.333
I probably would've
described myself, first,

00:10:31.333 --> 00:10:33.450
because of my visual identity in the

00:10:33.450 --> 00:10:36.090
sense that I have dwarfism,
that I'm a little person,

00:10:36.090 --> 00:10:37.690
I'm a disabled woman.

00:10:37.690 --> 00:10:40.210
Whilst I still use that language,

00:10:40.210 --> 00:10:43.683
I'd probably talk about my
interests and my work more now,

00:10:43.683 --> 00:10:48.683
not because those aren't
defined by my disability,

00:10:48.730 --> 00:10:49.650
because they are.

00:10:49.650 --> 00:10:52.178
I understood that when
people looked at me,

00:10:52.178 --> 00:10:54.690
they had assumptions over
what I could and couldn't do

00:10:54.690 --> 00:10:56.450
based on my aesthetic.

00:10:56.450 --> 00:10:59.070
And I knew within my own instincts that

00:10:59.070 --> 00:11:01.019
fashion had this power
to tell a new story,

00:11:01.019 --> 00:11:02.670
one that I had agency over,

00:11:02.670 --> 00:11:04.600
because I'm giving you permission

00:11:04.600 --> 00:11:07.990
to enter into my world, to learn who I am,

00:11:07.990 --> 00:11:12.124
and yet despite knowing the
power of fashion to do that,

00:11:12.124 --> 00:11:15.640
I never felt represented or seen

00:11:15.640 --> 00:11:20.040
or able to buy the clothes
that I felt comfortable in.

00:11:20.040 --> 00:11:22.680
The vulnerability that we've shared

00:11:22.680 --> 00:11:25.020
with each other in private moments

00:11:25.020 --> 00:11:30.020
has really emboldened me on my
own purpose in this industry.

00:11:30.443 --> 00:11:34.860
And I was someone who kind
of put my own identity

00:11:34.860 --> 00:11:36.047
in the background.

00:11:36.047 --> 00:11:38.630
Some of the challenge
around visible identities

00:11:38.630 --> 00:11:41.525
is that as much as we try to pocket them,

00:11:41.525 --> 00:11:44.020
the world stills sees us as that.

00:11:44.020 --> 00:11:46.160
I may not describe myself as disabled,

00:11:46.160 --> 00:11:49.300
but the world sees me as disabled,

00:11:49.300 --> 00:11:51.870
the fashion system sees me as disabled

00:11:51.870 --> 00:11:55.599
and me not having pride around that

00:11:55.599 --> 00:11:58.100
gives them permission to own whatever

00:11:58.100 --> 00:12:00.680
narrative they think
disability exists with them.

00:12:00.680 --> 00:12:02.700
I imagine that's the
same with being Muslim,

00:12:02.700 --> 00:12:05.328
being gay, being an immigrant.

00:12:05.328 --> 00:12:10.130
Tell me what happened after "Voices".

00:12:10.130 --> 00:12:11.323
Afterwards?

00:12:11.323 --> 00:12:16.323
I felt vulnerable because
I had exposed myself,

00:12:17.439 --> 00:12:18.920
personally,

00:12:18.920 --> 00:12:19.940
but also professionally.

00:12:19.940 --> 00:12:22.690
I had just told the industry
I knew what they needed to do.

00:12:22.690 --> 00:12:23.523
Did I?

00:12:23.523 --> 00:12:24.983
I had no idea.

00:12:24.983 --> 00:12:26.213
I haven't since then.

00:12:27.577 --> 00:12:29.380
And what happened afterwards was

00:12:30.560 --> 00:12:32.535
more and more conversations,

00:12:32.535 --> 00:12:37.535
initially with individuals,
then with friends and companies.

00:12:38.949 --> 00:12:43.580
It was appearing on stages
with microphone in my hand,

00:12:43.580 --> 00:12:45.480
going to fashion shows.

00:12:45.480 --> 00:12:47.740
It was incredible.

00:12:47.740 --> 00:12:51.187
Five years ago, if you were
to ask me what success was,

00:12:51.187 --> 00:12:53.982
I would've said the
availability of clothes.

00:12:53.982 --> 00:12:55.633
My vision has widened.

00:12:57.340 --> 00:13:01.130
I really do think the availability
of product is important

00:13:02.250 --> 00:13:04.485
because I think it's more important

00:13:04.485 --> 00:13:09.485
that we are hiring disabled
people within these companies.

00:13:09.600 --> 00:13:10.950
That's what I wanna change.

00:13:13.990 --> 00:13:16.230
Voices like Sinéad's matter,

00:13:16.230 --> 00:13:18.650
but how do we measure progress?

00:13:18.650 --> 00:13:21.770
One challenge is the lack of robust data.

00:13:21.770 --> 00:13:23.950
In the key European fashion economies of

00:13:23.950 --> 00:13:26.100
France, Italy, and Germany,

00:13:26.100 --> 00:13:28.919
companies cannot collect
personal data on identity

00:13:28.919 --> 00:13:31.610
due to national privacy laws.

00:13:31.610 --> 00:13:33.700
The U.K. goes the furthest,

00:13:33.700 --> 00:13:36.300
asking its citizens each Census to

00:13:36.300 --> 00:13:38.920
volunteer their racial, physical,

00:13:38.920 --> 00:13:41.073
sexual, and gender identities.

00:13:42.899 --> 00:13:45.150
So to continue our discussion,

00:13:45.150 --> 00:13:47.890
Sinéad and I meet with two
leaders in British fashion

00:13:47.890 --> 00:13:49.733
to share their own experiences.

00:13:50.800 --> 00:13:53.380
June Sarpong is a broadcaster, activist,

00:13:53.380 --> 00:13:56.580
author, and the first
director of creative diversity

00:13:56.580 --> 00:13:57.803
at the BBC.

00:13:58.660 --> 00:14:03.190
Jamie Gill is CEO of luxury
fashion brand Roksanda.

00:14:03.190 --> 00:14:05.490
Both are non-executive directors

00:14:05.490 --> 00:14:07.160
of the British Fashion Council,

00:14:07.160 --> 00:14:11.103
appointed in the wake of the
"Black Lives Matter" protests.

00:14:11.103 --> 00:14:14.180
June and Jamie, I just
wanted to start very quickly

00:14:15.315 --> 00:14:17.303
maybe with you first, June.

00:14:18.420 --> 00:14:23.330
How do you identify and
what is your personal story?

00:14:23.330 --> 00:14:27.070
My Britishness in a way is a sort of

00:14:27.070 --> 00:14:29.660
the link for me to the world, you know?

00:14:29.660 --> 00:14:33.010
It's a wonderful identity to be able

00:14:33.010 --> 00:14:34.570
to take with you globally.

00:14:34.570 --> 00:14:37.087
Everybody sort of
connects with Britishness.

00:14:37.087 --> 00:14:40.470
I would say my Ghanaian in culture

00:14:40.470 --> 00:14:41.900
for sure is my foundation.

00:14:41.900 --> 00:14:44.070
That's my roots.

00:14:44.070 --> 00:14:49.070
And then my race is something that I am

00:14:49.380 --> 00:14:54.270
so immersed in and with and also

00:14:54.270 --> 00:14:57.230
impacts my experience in so many ways.

00:14:57.230 --> 00:14:58.567
Jamie, how 'bout you?

00:14:58.567 --> 00:15:02.003
How do you identify?
Identify.

00:15:02.003 --> 00:15:05.896
I would identify myself as a

00:15:05.896 --> 00:15:09.870
British-Indian gay man, I think.

00:15:09.870 --> 00:15:11.430
And I'd add on Londoner

00:15:11.430 --> 00:15:12.740
I think to that as well.
Yes!

00:15:12.740 --> 00:15:13.911
I forgot that.

00:15:13.911 --> 00:15:14.744
That was one thing, I
was thinking about it,

00:15:14.744 --> 00:15:15.660
I was like, yeah, we say British,

00:15:15.660 --> 00:15:17.350
but I think there's, you
know, as we all know,

00:15:17.350 --> 00:15:20.710
there's a culture of being from London

00:15:22.056 --> 00:15:24.199
that I very much champion and I think

00:15:24.199 --> 00:15:27.460
growing up my whole life and coming from

00:15:27.460 --> 00:15:29.080
a working class background as well

00:15:29.080 --> 00:15:31.370
and growing up in the Midlands and being

00:15:31.370 --> 00:15:33.033
a minority on so many regards,

00:15:33.033 --> 00:15:36.381
I saw those as just such hindrances.

00:15:36.381 --> 00:15:38.670
Such hindrances that I carried with me,

00:15:38.670 --> 00:15:42.950
that weighed me down, that
didn't allow me to be free.

00:15:42.950 --> 00:15:46.180
So we're here talking about belonging

00:15:46.180 --> 00:15:50.440
in the fashion industry and
you both, all of us, actually,

00:15:50.440 --> 00:15:51.634
were outsiders.

00:15:51.634 --> 00:15:52.467
Oh yeah!

00:15:52.467 --> 00:15:53.300
All of us.

00:15:53.300 --> 00:15:56.506
None of us came up through
a traditional path.

00:15:56.506 --> 00:15:57.339
No.

00:15:57.339 --> 00:16:00.520
But we've all found our way
somehow into this industry.

00:16:00.520 --> 00:16:02.450
Some people outside might think

00:16:02.450 --> 00:16:05.340
we don't have a diversity
problem, for that regard,

00:16:05.340 --> 00:16:09.700
but operationally, the
people from any different

00:16:09.700 --> 00:16:12.870
kind of background I think
is very, very minimal

00:16:12.870 --> 00:16:15.340
and I think working in a
kind of smaller business,

00:16:15.340 --> 00:16:16.790
but if we were to look bigger

00:16:16.790 --> 00:16:19.476
and we would look at
listed companies out there

00:16:19.476 --> 00:16:21.190
even within the kind of fashion space

00:16:21.190 --> 00:16:22.700
is what acts sees sweet.

00:16:22.700 --> 00:16:24.320
It is nonexistent.

00:16:24.320 --> 00:16:26.930
But are we at a point now where we need

00:16:26.930 --> 00:16:29.040
measurables, KPIs?

00:16:29.040 --> 00:16:32.652
Like what does success look
like when it's delivered?

00:16:32.652 --> 00:16:36.450
I don't think we have an
example of success yet,

00:16:36.450 --> 00:16:38.250
and that's the issue.

00:16:38.250 --> 00:16:41.500
So we're asking people
to imagine something

00:16:41.500 --> 00:16:44.030
that perhaps in any industry, actually,

00:16:44.030 --> 00:16:46.810
has never really existed,
at least not at scale.

00:16:46.810 --> 00:16:50.170
The first thing to do is to connect

00:16:50.170 --> 00:16:54.000
with the most disenfranchised group

00:16:54.000 --> 00:16:57.405
within your organization and ask them

00:16:57.405 --> 00:17:00.870
what their experience
is of your organization.

00:17:00.870 --> 00:17:03.070
That's gonna tell you
everything about your culture.

00:17:03.070 --> 00:17:04.980
Well, it raises a really
interesting question

00:17:04.980 --> 00:17:06.490
about our industry as a whole, though,

00:17:06.490 --> 00:17:11.110
is like this industry was
designed around exclusivity.

00:17:11.110 --> 00:17:12.410
It is.

00:17:12.410 --> 00:17:14.944
So is there a way of reconciling that

00:17:14.944 --> 00:17:18.030
exclusivity in terms
of prize, or scarcity,

00:17:18.030 --> 00:17:21.090
or access, or being in the room with

00:17:21.090 --> 00:17:23.780
inclusion and belonging and equity?

00:17:23.780 --> 00:17:24.613
Yes!

00:17:24.613 --> 00:17:26.840
And that what it should've been

00:17:26.840 --> 00:17:29.990
designed around is aspiration,

00:17:29.990 --> 00:17:32.800
in that it's about all of us wanting

00:17:32.800 --> 00:17:36.170
to be the best version of ourselves.

00:17:36.170 --> 00:17:38.008
If you look at accessories,

00:17:38.008 --> 00:17:40.730
accessories are about aspiration,

00:17:40.730 --> 00:17:43.180
accessories are the entry point for

00:17:43.180 --> 00:17:46.330
people who can't afford the actual clothes

00:17:46.330 --> 00:17:47.760
et cetera, et cetera.

00:17:47.760 --> 00:17:49.600
They knew they had to do that because

00:17:49.600 --> 00:17:51.200
of the financial benefits.

00:17:51.200 --> 00:17:53.475
Why can't we apply that same thinking

00:17:53.475 --> 00:17:55.700
for everything that
we're talking about here?

00:17:55.700 --> 00:17:58.810
Amongst this monoculture
that fashion still is,

00:17:58.810 --> 00:18:00.920
especially on the business side,

00:18:00.920 --> 00:18:05.920
how do we also do it in a way
that people aren't so scared?

00:18:06.490 --> 00:18:09.791
When we talk about the
changes that are needed,

00:18:09.791 --> 00:18:12.087
we need to be really frank that

00:18:12.087 --> 00:18:15.623
for those who have been used to privilege,

00:18:16.706 --> 00:18:19.090
equity feels like oppression.
Like oppression, yes.

00:18:19.090 --> 00:18:19.970
Cindy Gallop.

00:18:19.970 --> 00:18:20.973
She's right!

00:18:23.310 --> 00:18:27.623
Because in order for
others to take up space,

00:18:28.780 --> 00:18:30.870
some have to retract.

00:18:30.870 --> 00:18:32.850
So that pattern of behavior that they've

00:18:32.850 --> 00:18:35.410
been practicing in terms
of what leadership is...

00:18:35.410 --> 00:18:36.243
Won't work anymore.

00:18:36.243 --> 00:18:37.290
No longer exists.

00:18:37.290 --> 00:18:39.360
So it's not that we need to replace

00:18:39.360 --> 00:18:41.810
every CEO with somebody
who looks different.

00:18:41.810 --> 00:18:43.540
It's not functional, nor it is practical,

00:18:43.540 --> 00:18:45.650
nor is it the way we need to move forward.

00:18:45.650 --> 00:18:48.430
We've got to do something visually,

00:18:48.430 --> 00:18:51.750
very externally projecting
that we are welcoming

00:18:51.750 --> 00:18:53.500
and there are roles available,

00:18:53.500 --> 00:18:56.500
at least from the operational side,

00:18:56.500 --> 00:19:00.060
like what is it like to
be an operations director

00:19:00.060 --> 00:19:02.210
of a fashion business and why does that

00:19:02.210 --> 00:19:05.290
skillset actually maybe lean in to

00:19:05.290 --> 00:19:07.790
what you think you want to be doing?

00:19:07.790 --> 00:19:10.670
So I think we do need to be
working on that as a collective.

00:19:10.670 --> 00:19:12.590
If we go back to the ways in which

00:19:12.590 --> 00:19:17.060
we have always done
things, what does it take?

00:19:17.060 --> 00:19:18.610
People to die?

00:19:18.610 --> 00:19:21.690
If we look at George Floyd,
if we look at the pandemic,

00:19:21.690 --> 00:19:26.690
it requires us to lose
people in order to change.

00:19:27.267 --> 00:19:30.673
The great thing is is that
between the four of us,

00:19:31.910 --> 00:19:34.913
we can probably change a
sizable portion of the world.

00:19:38.830 --> 00:19:40.170
With that on our minds,

00:19:40.170 --> 00:19:42.833
Sinéad and I head over
to Proportion London,

00:19:43.860 --> 00:19:46.190
one of the UK's largest manufacturers

00:19:46.190 --> 00:19:48.373
of mannequins for the retail industry.

00:19:50.330 --> 00:19:53.230
Today, Sinéad is having the final casting

00:19:53.230 --> 00:19:55.063
to complete a mannequin of her body,

00:19:55.063 --> 00:19:57.370
which can be used as a model for other

00:19:57.370 --> 00:20:00.668
little people to be better
represented in fashion.

00:20:00.668 --> 00:20:01.830
Hello!

00:20:01.830 --> 00:20:03.180
Gaetano, this is Imran.

00:20:03.180 --> 00:20:04.887
Imran, this is Gaetano.

00:20:04.887 --> 00:20:05.720
Hi.

00:20:05.720 --> 00:20:06.660
Nice to meet you.
How are you?

00:20:06.660 --> 00:20:07.493
I am all right.

00:20:07.493 --> 00:20:08.840
Thank you for having us.

00:20:08.840 --> 00:20:09.763
It's such a treat.

00:20:10.621 --> 00:20:12.954
You wanna put this one on.

00:20:14.070 --> 00:20:15.905
This is gonna be the most glamorous fit

00:20:15.905 --> 00:20:18.533
of the entire fashion show.

00:20:18.533 --> 00:20:22.386
Like couture has nothing on us, Gaetano.

00:20:22.386 --> 00:20:25.701
Sinéad, this is the second
time you're doing this, right?

00:20:25.701 --> 00:20:26.534
Yeah.

00:20:26.534 --> 00:20:27.930
So do you wanna tell us a little bit

00:20:27.930 --> 00:20:31.013
about how this mannequin
situation happened?

00:20:32.020 --> 00:20:33.695
So the National Museums of Scotland

00:20:33.695 --> 00:20:35.550
were putting together this exhibition

00:20:35.550 --> 00:20:36.660
called "Body Beautiful".

00:20:36.660 --> 00:20:39.720
They were the first ever
cultural institution

00:20:39.720 --> 00:20:42.270
to do an exhibition with
diversity in fashion

00:20:42.270 --> 00:20:43.890
and when it came to disability,

00:20:43.890 --> 00:20:45.526
they asked if they could
borrow some of my clothes.

00:20:45.526 --> 00:20:48.730
And one of those items
was the trench coat,

00:20:48.730 --> 00:20:49.753
which I had actually saved from the

00:20:49.753 --> 00:20:51.620
"Voices of Fashion" shoot and I said,

00:20:51.620 --> 00:20:53.640
how are you gonna display my clothes?

00:20:53.640 --> 00:20:54.900
And the initial suggestion was that

00:20:54.900 --> 00:20:56.659
they would hang them from the ceiling.

00:20:56.659 --> 00:20:59.700
A lovely idea, but I asked
how was everything else

00:20:59.700 --> 00:21:00.830
being displayed?

00:21:00.830 --> 00:21:02.050
And they just said mannequins.

00:21:02.050 --> 00:21:03.660
I said, okay, great.

00:21:03.660 --> 00:21:06.903
How do we get a mannequin
made for my clothes?

00:21:12.787 --> 00:21:15.150
I can't tickle her right now, right?

00:21:15.150 --> 00:21:15.983
No.

00:21:17.200 --> 00:21:20.373
So after this mannequin is
done, what's gonna happen?

00:21:20.373 --> 00:21:22.060
My hope is that first,

00:21:22.060 --> 00:21:23.576
we kind of tackle the education system

00:21:23.576 --> 00:21:25.900
and that we're able to collaborate with

00:21:25.900 --> 00:21:28.113
Central Saint Martins, Parsons, FIT,

00:21:28.113 --> 00:21:30.180
the National College of
Art and Design in Ireland,

00:21:30.180 --> 00:21:32.700
to make these available for students

00:21:32.700 --> 00:21:36.380
because if we have
mannequins, all of a sudden,

00:21:36.380 --> 00:21:40.087
fit becomes a different
conversation, and from then,

00:21:40.087 --> 00:21:42.470
it would be great to see this mannequin

00:21:42.470 --> 00:21:45.684
in retail spaces visually,
but we need product first.

00:21:45.684 --> 00:21:47.170
And you don't have anything to

00:21:47.170 --> 00:21:48.555
put on the mannequin yet.

00:21:48.555 --> 00:21:49.388
No.

00:21:49.388 --> 00:21:52.480
So putting clothes that
may fit is one thing,

00:21:52.480 --> 00:21:53.950
but it needs to be in the tailors,

00:21:53.950 --> 00:21:55.723
it needs to be in design studios,

00:21:55.723 --> 00:21:57.370
and we need to be looking at this

00:21:57.370 --> 00:21:59.300
as a way in which to get it into CAD also

00:21:59.300 --> 00:22:00.923
and thinking about the measurements.

00:22:08.888 --> 00:22:09.721
Amazing.

00:22:10.569 --> 00:22:13.851
You see that and went mhm.
Yeah.

00:22:13.851 --> 00:22:15.684
Just a little bit longer than this.

00:22:15.684 --> 00:22:16.851
Yeah.
Yeah.

00:22:18.217 --> 00:22:21.968
My mannequin is a composite of me.

00:22:21.968 --> 00:22:25.913
My lumps, my bumps, my curves, me.

00:22:26.790 --> 00:22:29.523
And when I first saw
the mannequin in person,

00:22:30.389 --> 00:22:33.000
I was really insecure about it because

00:22:33.000 --> 00:22:35.710
it didn't look like any of
the mannequins that I had seen

00:22:35.710 --> 00:22:38.390
and I was almost joking about
the size and the measurements

00:22:38.390 --> 00:22:40.263
and I was uncomfortable with it,

00:22:41.940 --> 00:22:43.750
but I'm not uncomfortable with me.

00:22:43.750 --> 00:22:47.020
It's the world making me feel like I don't

00:22:47.020 --> 00:22:50.270
conform to everything else, and actually,

00:22:50.270 --> 00:22:53.363
it's that discomfort that
proves the importance of this.

00:22:54.200 --> 00:22:56.750
As soon as you told me
that this was happening,

00:22:56.750 --> 00:22:59.860
I wanted to be apart of
it and just witness it

00:22:59.860 --> 00:23:02.690
because it felt pretty monumental.

00:23:02.690 --> 00:23:05.680
It wasn't until I saw all of
these mannequins around me

00:23:05.680 --> 00:23:07.701
that I realized that in a place like this

00:23:07.701 --> 00:23:10.838
where these body shapes
and these body types

00:23:10.838 --> 00:23:14.890
are then replicated over,
and over, and over again

00:23:14.890 --> 00:23:19.890
and show up in retail
stores and in design studios

00:23:20.196 --> 00:23:23.530
and in schools that that's why we

00:23:23.530 --> 00:23:28.060
all develop these ideas of
what body shape should be.

00:23:28.060 --> 00:23:30.070
And it wasn't until seeing you

00:23:30.070 --> 00:23:33.683
in this space that that
was reinforced for me.

00:23:35.550 --> 00:23:37.340
Marginalized communities are not

00:23:37.340 --> 00:23:40.060
looking for special
treatment from fashion,

00:23:40.060 --> 00:23:42.530
they're looking for equal treatment.

00:23:42.530 --> 00:23:44.610
There are certainly powerful, moral,

00:23:44.610 --> 00:23:47.340
and business arguments for doing so,

00:23:47.340 --> 00:23:49.220
but the question remains,

00:23:49.220 --> 00:23:53.613
how prepared is the fashion
industry to take real action?

